I've been lurking for about two weeks now and this is my first post to
this list. Because it's my first post, I'm including my PGP key at the
end of this message.
 
On 1993 April 8, John McPherson asked:
 JM> "If you could have one of the above, now, which would you 
 JM> pick?"
 
Here's my list, in order:
 
B. Maximum Socio-economic Freedom
C. Maximum Technological Access
E. Space Activities
 
F. Meetings
D. Optimal Self-Transformation
A. Personal Immortality
 
I see C. and E. as being very strongly supporting of B., and this is
the main reason I rank them second and third. I rank D. and A. low for
the fairly unextropian reason that I'm confident about the work that
other people are doing on them, and also for the fairly extropian
reason that I feel my own abilities are much more useful for advancing
B. than D. or A.
 
A. would probably be much higher on my list than it is if I were
thirty years older than I am. As it is I take a fairly standard
Durk-and-Sandy influenced formula of vitamins and minerals and such
stuff, and leave it at that for now.
 
When I was a teenager, I was active in the local chapters of L5 where
I lived, and E. was definitely at the top of the list. After a while
(and most especially after L5 was consumed by a group that sees its
primary role as lobbying for NASA funding) I came to see that working
for C. and B. was the best short-term way for me to advance E.
 
I've been an anarchist for as long as I've been politically literate.
I started out as the naive left-wing typically adolescent anarchist
(reference: LeGuin's The Dispossessed) but was fortunate in running
across libertarian stuff by Leonard Read while I was still in high
school, and reading Ayn Rand and Henry Hazlitt, who at least kept me
confused and open-minded about the private property thing until I met
Anton Sherwood, who handed me a copy of Friedman's tMoF, in which for
the first time I found a coherent system of political ideas that I was
entirely comfortable with. Before that I had a pragmatic view that the
power of property interests was at odds with (and somewhat preferable
to) the power of state interests, and that increasing the power of
property interests was an effective way of tearing down the state. Now
I don't even see this as a compromise anymore, since I'm no longer
uncomfortable with the "power" of property interests.
 
So now I'm mainly interested in B., and I'm interested in C.
(especially crypto and communications) as a way to protect our
activities from interference by the state and in E. as a way to
provide a place for a stateless society without having to do all the
hard work of evaporating all the various governments on this planet.
 
And I suppose I've been taking too narrow a view of D. since I'm
always looking for books and ideas and sifting the net and looking
critically at my own behavior and reactions and modifying them
according to whether I approve of them or not. In a real, everyday
way, I might have to admit that D. has the biggest effect on the way I
live my life. But most of my applications of D. are not the exotica
that many people associate with extropian activities.
 
