Date: Wed, 14 Apr 93 8:50:23 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Singularity Studies

Nick Szabo makes some good points about the uncertainties in 
Dani Eder's extrapolation to a Singularity near 2035, and
whether there can ever be a "true" singularity or not, I
feel the concept is a valid one -- a point in the future
beyond which life will be so different that little if any
of it would make sense to us today.

Despite the inherent difficulties of such projection, I do
find it interesting.  One place to begin a serious course
of "singularity study" would be to list the possible paths
that could lead to a singularity.  I'll start (note that
these are NOT mutually exclusive or independent):

* The Dani Eder scenario.  Computer capabilities continue on
  their exponential capability curve.  Eventually, artificial
  scientists and engineers will be as capable as human ones.
  Soon thereafter they will be much more numerous and capable
  than humans, leading to an explosion in technological progress,
  altering life in fundamental ways.

* The Hans Moravec scenario.  Advances in computers and robotics
  will lead to the ability to upload our consciousnesses into
  superior containers, providing a variety of capability
  enhancements, altering life in fundamental ways.

* The Vernor Vinge scenario.  Neural interfaces and increases in
  communication technologies will lead to a network in which people's
  nervous systems are in effect directly linked, changing capabilities
  and personal identities in fundamental ways.  Then, for some unknown
  reason, everybody disappears :-).

* The Eric Drexler scenario.  A concerted effort will lead to the
  technological capability of controlling matter at the atomic
  scale; self-replicating assembly machines will make the capability
  ubiquitous; the resulting range of technological capabilities will
  alter life in fundamental ways.

* The ??? Genetic Engineering scenario.  By understanding the biological
  machinery of which we are constructed, we will acquire the ability
  to alter the genetic program, changing the human body and brain.
  For those already born, the possibility exists for surgical techniques
  and gene therapy.

Help this infant academic discipline!  Add more scenarios!  For
the more methodical researchers out there, flesh out the various
scenarios -- of particular use would be a Drake-type equation
for each scenario, to help judge its likelihood and timetable.

