 szabo@techbook.com  (Nick Szabo) writes:
>There is no garuntee that CPU speed will keep doubling every
>two years over the next 40 years.  We may find the going tougher
>as we slog into scales where quantum mechanics takes over.  This
>is only 2-3 orders of magnitude away, while at least 6 orders of 
>magnitude are needed to match brain thruput as determined by
>the 100 bits/(synapse*sec).  As a result, the S-curve of CPU
>speed (the performance of most technologies follows an S-curve) 
>may start flattening out, delaying 6 orders of magnitude by 
>many decades if not indefinitely.

 The growth rate of single-processor speeds may well slow down soon, but that
does not imply that effective CPU-speeds won't continue double every 2 years.
Virtually unlimited speedups can be achieved via matrices of parallel
processors. The only real reason parallelism isn't taking off yet is that
most programming languages encourage programmers to specify sequential
ordering in their code much more often than sequential order is needed.
 Once programming languages and styles adapt to parallelism, the limits
to CPU-speed growth will be economics, not physics.

