Date: Wed, 14 Apr 1993 10:17:44 +0000
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Unconsionableness

Tim of the firmament(t) writes:

>My reading of Black's Law Dictionary leads me to believe that a contract is
>held "unconscionable" when allegedly no one in their right mind would enter
>into it.

Black's also refers to contracts that are unconscionable due to procedural
abuses--not just due to substantive provisions that are oppressive or
grossly unreasonable.  BLACKS LAW DICTIONARY (6th ed, 1990) p 1524.

>I'm curious to know whether this is correct.

I think this pretty much jibes with what I've heard elsewhere, though it
does not mention contracts that are reasonable for both parties but void
due to public policy considerations (such as contracts to murder).

Keep in mind, though, that while it's a standard reference in law, Black's
sometimes falls flat.  Consider this comment under battery:  "The actual
offer to use force to the injury of another person is *assault*; the use of
it is *battery*, which always includes assault."  Id p 153.  This is
inaccurate, because battery can take place w/o assault if the victim has no
apprehension of the offensive touching.  But it may be most fair to regard
this not as a blatant falsehood but instead as an almost unavoidable
failure to include all of the law's subtle twists and turns.

>If so, then I find it arbitrary
>and invasive, and completely out of place in the best of all possible legal
>systems.

It does seem to invite abuse.  But keep in mind that even the most perfect
set of rules for ensuring that contracts are procedurally sound will have
loopholes.  You might think of the doctrine of unconscionability as an
equitable cure for contracts that violate the spirit, but not the letter,
of such rules.  

>My understanding is that this standard is commonly thought to be why such
>contracts as those made under duress and slavery contracts are unenforceable,
>but that this is false, too.  Slavery and price fixing contracts are
>unenforceable due to the absence of receipt of due consideration by both
>parties.  If contracts under duress are unenforceable for reasons other than
>unconscionableness, then why?  What legal concept applies?

I've never heard of slavery or price fixing found unenforceable for lack of
consideration, and don't see how they could be.  Consideration can
typically be shown by virtually any exchange of goods--a bean or button
will suffice.  It tests assent, not equality of exchange, since you cannot
give consideration if you are unconscious.  (But maybe you are referring to
*adequate* consideration tests . . .) I would think that slavery contracts
are held to be unconscionable because they are grossly unjust to one of the
parties and void under public policy.  Price fixing only fails under the
latter test.

>On its face, a right-wing Christian could call contracts to employ homosexuals
>"unconscionable," and anti-porn feminists could call the contracts that Play-
>boy's models enter into "unconscionable."  

Yes, that seems to be true--under the doctrine that refuses to enforce
contracts that violate public policy.  Note that some feminists would also
include pornography agreements under the banner of contracts that are
grossly unfair to the parties.

>Is there any justification at all
>for this standard of contract-evaluation?  If so, can it be construed so as
>to exclude such clearly undesirable outcomes in a principled way?

Sure, there is a justification (see above) but not one I think either of us
would accept!  It gives such outcomes in a principled manner, too, but not
on principles that either of us would accept, either.


