Date: Wed, 14 Apr 93 10:14:38 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: EVOL: Extropian Challenge (An Unscientific Proof)

Analysis of the Huntsville, AL Telephone Directory

by Dani Eder

The December 1992 Huntsville, AL Telephone directory has 228 1/4
pages of residence listings, with 4 columns per page, for a total
of 913 columns of listings.  A sample count of 2 columns shows an
average of 85 entries per column, so there is an estimated 77,600
entries in the directory.  A count of the first 5 pages of the
directory reveals 413 surnames.  Projecting to the full directory,
we get an estimated 18,850 surnames.  Thus, the average surname
has about 4.1 entries.

Surnames are not distributed evenly.  A search was made of all
surnames with >= 0.8 columns of entries.  This represents about
0.1% of the directory per surname.  I will define this set as
'common surnames'.  There were 143 common surnames found.

The seven most common surnames (Brown, Davis, Johnson, Jones,
Moore, Smith, and Williams) together accounted for 51.1 columns,
or 5.6% of the directory.  The next group of names, havings between
3.0 and 4.9 columns of entries (Anderson, Clark(e), Hall, Jackson,
Martin, Miller, Robinson, Taylor, Thomas, Thompson, Walker, White,
and Wilson) total 43 columns.  Thus the top 20 names prvide
10.3% of all surnames.

The next group, 2.0-2.9 columns has 16 members (Adams, Allen, 
Baker, Campbell, Carter, Green(e) Harris, Hill, Lee, Lewis,
Mitchell, Parker, Phillips, Stewart, Turner, and Wright).  They
total 38.0 columns, and the remainder, 107 surnames, comprise
117.6 columns.  Therefore the entire set of common surnames
totals 249.7 columns, or 27.3% of all surnames.  The remaining
18,700 names therefore share 56,375 entries, or an average
of 3 entries per name.

In considering the evolution of surnames, it is not correct to
consider the population of the US as a whole.  People frequently
choose their spouses from the local area in which they live
(within a few hours distance), since these are the people with
which they most often interact.  It was recently reported that
about 1/3 of the US population has moved from the state of their
birth, so a model of name evolution should reflect local choice,
with a mixing rate of about 1% per annum from out-of-area
populations.

Surnames with low incidence, such as one holder per city, will
often disappear in a few generations, but in the mean time
the mixing rate from outside the area will replenish the set
of names, since many of the newcomers will have low-incidence names.


