From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Extropian Priorities

In response to Mr. Szabo's message:

Today's type of chips are good for about another factor of 500
improvement through reduction of feature size (from 0.8 micron to
0.1 micron).  When you reduce feature size, performance goes
up about as the cube (you square the number of circuit elements,
and the clock rate also goes up linearly).  Of that is coupled
with productivtity increases through more automated factories,
I would expect we could easily get a total 10^4 improvement in
bis/sec/$ without any new technology being implemented.

Already people are considering how to USE the quantum effects
that appear at scales below 0.1 micron.  If ways to use these
quantum effects prove out, we have another factor of 100-1000
improvement available, which puts us right at my projected
'singularity'.

Note that I use the term 'singularity' not to refer to a time
of infinte progress.  Instead, I recognize the 'S-curve' effect
as it applies to individual technologies, and then extrapolate
that effect to technology as a whole.  I expect there to be
a time when progress is limited by fundamental constants of
the universe (plancks', speed of light).  Between now and then
will be a period of fastest progress, the nearly vertical
part of the S-curve.  This period of fastest progress will occur
once in history, hence is 'singular'.  The difference between
an asymptotic appraoch to infinity and a levelling off only
become appreciable on the far side of the singularity.  From
where we stand on the near side, the curves are indistinguishable.

I personally define the near edge of the singularity as that
time at which life expectancy begins to increase at over
1 year per year, and that time after which human-equaivalent
computer power is available.  This means that the chances to
live a long time become very much greater than before.  

I see a discontinuity in the answer to 'how long will you live'
between those who are 75 years old today (~10 years), and
those 30 years old today (maybe 1000 years), and the break
comes when the lifespan increases at over 1 year per year,
and the age at death stops being a cluster around the 80
year mark, and spreads upwards drastically.

Dani 

