From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Singularity Studies

Derek Zahn:

> [Dani Eder's scenario]
>   Eventually, artificial
>   scientists and engineers will be as capable as human ones.

This may already be true, it's just that we haven't learned to
use computers to anywhere near their full capability -- at current
speeds.  Furthermore, as another poster pointed out, CPU speedup
itself may be primarily a function of changing the way programmers 
write software to promote parallelization -- a social and software
change.

> * The Hans Moravec scenario.  Advances in computers and robotics
>   will lead to the ability to upload our consciousnesses...

This depends much more on our ability to simulate neurons sufficiently
well than it does on raw computing power.  Simulating 
synapses, which Dani Eder estimated have 100 bits/(sec*synapse) 
processing power, may require >>100 bits/(sec*synapse) and thus
not be economical until computer hardware itself is radically
redesigned.  Robot capability is also largely a matter of software.
There is also the problem of simulating or losing hormones, gonads,
sexuality, appetite, etc. which dominate human motivation.

> * The Vernor Vinge scenario.  Neural interfaces and increases in
>   communication technologies will lead to a network in which people's
>   nervous systems are in effect directly linked

This probably requires redesigning the neural interfaces themselves,
including how the brain interprets stimuli, since currently we are 
designed to interface with the world and with other neural systems 
through specific channels (eg language).  Major discovery and design
problem, and also not clear there is significant benefit to be gained.

> * The Eric Drexler scenario.

Nanotechnology is highly dependent on software issues -- how well
can molecules be simulated, how much of the complexity can we
automate out of the design process (eg with "molecular compilation", 
GP, etc.), etc.  Cell repair machines cannot bring about major life
extension without detailed understanding of what goes wrong during cancer, 
Alzheimers, Parkinson's, depression, aging, freezing & thawing damage, 
etc. and why.   Self-replicating machines don't help unless they
can also manufacture a wide variety of useful products that
biotechnology cannot. 

> * The ??? Genetic Engineering scenario. 

This already exists.  If you want to get gene therapy, you have to
apply personally to the NIH which approves or rejects the cases one
by one.  There is a major social problem in establishing genetic
therapy for "enhancement" problems like reversing aging.  Solutions
may involve social innovations like offshore hospitals and health 
provider reputation systems.  And while it is easy to target and alter 
specific genes (by releasing custom-made viruses into the body,
no less), it is often not easy to predict the effects of the alteration
on metabolsim, structure, etc. without great improvement in  our 
understanding of biology.  Biovirus hacking also presents the possibility 
of near-term bio-graygoo catastrophes, which make all these other
scenarios less likely and later.

> Help this infant academic discipline!  Add more scenarios!  

The Imaging Scenario: the resolution of STM, AFM, MRI, X-ray
crystollography, etc. improve quickly, allowing us to decipher protein 
structure, get animated pictures of ribosome function, etc.
Once we find out the real problems and their causes, advanced biotech 
is sufficient to curing cancer, aging, freezing damage, etc.

The Automated Discovery Scenario: Genetic programming implements
a combination of symbolic regression, statistical analysis and
theorem-proving, and is applied to real-world science problems: the 
mass of undigested biology data from the Imaging Scenario, the anomolous 
data in physics (neutrino deficit, homogenously distribted gamma-ray sources 
discovered by GRO, possible cold fusion, etc.), developing efficient
quantum-mechanical simulations of chemical reactions, etc.
Scientists immediately see the value of automating what they now perceive 
to be the most creative and important part of science -- discovering
things.  Software issues aside, I think you can see the social problems 
with this. :-)

Digital market scenario: It turns out that communism and
today's capitalism have a negligible difference in 
utility compared to tommorrow's digital capitalism, due to the
latter's lowering of transaction costs by dozens of orders of magnitude.  
This allows a vast variety of new futures, synthetic assets, 
personal income shares, crypto-anarchy, etc.  This in turn stimulates 
massive economic growth, quick adaptation of workers to booming new 
businesses of software, biotech, etc. and makes all these other
scenarios more likely sooner.

The smart drug & CD-ROM scenario: we discover a smart drug that
can be manfactured for a few dollars per dosage-year, which raises
everybody's IQ to 150 (along suitably diverse dimensions, no IQ
flames please).  Combined with cheap CD-ROM libraries in the
3rd World, the number of highly skilled engineers, scientists,
financiers, etc. increases by an order of magnitude per decade in 
the near future.  This in turn makes these other scenarios more likely
sooner.

Learning to use what we've already got scenario: computers can do
most of what engineers do even now -- arithmetic, most engineering
math, statistical analysis, blueprint drawing, layout of
chips and PCBs, search of vast libraries, etc. much faster than engineers.  
Designers, inventors, decision makers, investors, etc. learn how to 
fully use today's computers to bootstrap their capability by 
several orders of magnitude.

> of particular use would be a Drake-type equation
> for each scenario, to help judge its likelihood and timetable.

Unfortuneately, every single scenario is highly dependent on 
unpredictable improvements in scientific knowledge, software and/or
social adaptation to new technology.  Like the Drake equation itself, 
many of the variables are highly uncertain by orders of magnitude.


