From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Singularity Studies

Nick Szabo responds to my scenario list -- I'm afraid I didn't
communicate very well what project I'm working toward -- a
list of qualitatively different "singularities".  The ones I
listed were restatements of others' (more or less) reasonable
extrapolations of current trends that can lead to a _severe_
warping of what life will be like, by different mechanisms:

  * computer-generated (therefore self-bootstrapping) technological progress
  * integration of individual nervous systems into a larger "whole"
  * large-scale transfer of consciousness to non-biological media
  * complete control of matter at atomic scale (space flight, cell repair, ...)
  * control of biological specifications and mechanisms for self-design
    (production of larger/more capable biological brains)

Nick makes some good comments about what stands in the way of achieving
these things, and points out that software may be the bottleneck rather
than hardware for many of the tasks.  This should factor in the Drake
equations somehow.

Another good point Nick makes is that most of these singularity
scenarios require willing participation by some fraction of society;
for the upload path (e.g.) that may be a problem.

I'd suggest that Nick's suggestions for new scenarios don't really
count -- instead they are enabling technologies for one or more
of the above scenarios.  As such the suggestions are quite good.
Also note that just having a cancer cure, aging cure, or whatever,
isn't sufficient to produce a singularity per se (maybe I could be
argued out of that position).

> Unfortuneately, every single scenario is highly dependent on 
> unpredictable improvements in scientific knowledge, software and/or
> social adaptation to new technology.  Like the Drake equation itself, 
> many of the variables are highly uncertain by orders of magnitude.

Well, yes, but that doesn't stop it from being fun.  Your imaginative
speculation about different paths to singularity makes me rethink
the Drake-equation-approach as an appropriate formalism for this
task.  Perhaps dependency graphs (Gantt charts?) would be more
appropriate.

derek

