Date: Wed, 14 Apr 93 13:35:33 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Singularity Studies

I agree with Nick Szabo that software and social context are key; the
time at which a typical computer has the same raw crunch abilities as
human brains is a poor indicator of anything important.

As I've said on this list before, I prefer the term "horizon" over
"singularity" to refer to the point in time where our current
prediction abilities seem to break down.  A horizon naturally recedes
as you approach it.  

"Singularity" should be reserved for points in time that will seem
special before, after, and during them.  So Dani Eder's use of the
term in the following seems appropriate:

>Note that I use the term 'singularity' not to refer to a time
>of infinte progress.  Instead, I recognize the 'S-curve' effect
>as it applies to individual technologies, and then extrapolate
>>that effect to technology as a whole.  I expect there to be
>a time when progress is limited by fundamental constants of
>the universe (plancks', speed of light).  Between now and then
>will be a period of fastest progress, the nearly vertical
>part of the S-curve.  This period of fastest progress will occur
>once in history, hence is 'singular'.  The difference between
>an asymptotic appraoch to infinity and a levelling off only
>become appreciable on the far side of the singularity.  From
>where we stand on the near side, the curves are indistinguishable.

I'm not sure there will ever be such a point in time, where overall
progress slows down for good, but if there is I think it is at least a
few centuries away.

>I personally define the near edge of the singularity as that
>time at which life expectancy begins to increase at over
>1 year per year, and that time after which human-equaivalent
>computer power is available.  This means that the chances to
>live a long time become very much greater than before.  

These are two different points in time, and need not be close to
either each other or to the above defined "singularity".  Also, after
uploading, and assuming life expectancy is taken as an average over
all copies of everyone, I'm not sure the first condition will ever apply.

Robin Hanson

